.TH SAMBA 8 "2026-10-05" "samba" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
samba \- pure-Go SMB2/SMB3 file server (zero-copy reads, multichannel)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B samba
[\fB\-c\fR \fIpath\fR | \fB\-\-config\fR \fIpath\fR]
[\fB\-\-check\fR]
[\fB\-\-dump\-config\fR]
[\fB\-\-list\-dialects\fR]
[\fB\-\-list\-ciphers\fR]
[\fB\-\-list\-interfaces\fR]
[\fB\-\-log\-level\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-\-listen\fR \fIaddr\fR]
[\fB\-\-workers\fR \fIn\fR]
[\fB\-V\fR | \fB\-\-version\fR]
[\fB\-h\fR | \fB\-\-help\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B samba
is a from-scratch SMB2/3 file server, an
.BR smbd (8)
replacement, written entirely in Go: no CGO, no \f(CWunsafe\fR. It listens on
TCP 445 only (direct TCP; there is no NetBIOS port 139, no RPC, and no
management API), and it serves every worker from its own
.BR SO_REUSEPORT
listener so the kernel spreads connections across CPU cores.
.PP
It speaks SMB 2.0.2 through 3.1.1 with NTLMv2 authentication, SMB2/3 signing,
SMB 3.1.1 preauth integrity, SMB3 multichannel,
SMB3 encryption (AES-128/256-GCM and AES-128/256-CCM), byte-range locks,
directory change notification, and read- and handle-caching leases.
.PP
Large unsigned reads never copy the file's data through userspace: the response
header is written and the kernel moves page-cache pages straight into the socket
with
.BR splice (2).
Reads that must be signed or encrypted, and reads inside a compound request,
take a buffered positional-read path instead.
.PP
At \fBlog_level\fR 1 or above the server logs a banner reporting every
capability it is about to advertise. The same report, from the same source, is
what
.B \-\-check
prints, and the negotiation facts are what the
.B \-\-list\-\fR
options print.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-c " " \fIpath\fR ", " \-\-config " " \fIpath\fR
Path to the TOML configuration file. Default: \fIsamba.toml\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-check
Validate the configuration and report everything it enables — the resolved
settings, every capability, the shares and the users — then exit. It builds the
full server context, so a configuration that cannot start (an unusable keytab, a
bad bind address, a malformed user database) fails here exactly as it would at
startup. Nothing is bound.
.TP
.B \-\-dump\-config
Print the resolved configuration as TOML, with passwords and NT hashes
redacted, and exit. Useful in a bug report; it is not a usable config file.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-dialects
Print the SMB dialects this build negotiates, newest first, with the revision
codes. No configuration is needed.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-ciphers
Print the SMB3 encryption ciphers (strongest first, the order \fBprefer_aes256\fR
applies) and the signature algorithms. No configuration is needed.
.TP
.B \-\-list\-interfaces
Print the multichannel interface advertisement after the \fBadvertise_only\fR
filter: the addresses, link speeds and capability bits a client uses to decide
how many channels to open.
.TP
.B \-\-log\-level " " \fIn\fR
Override \fBlog_level\fR: 0 (warn), 1 (info) or 2 (debug).
.TP
.B \-\-listen " " \fIaddr\fR
Override \fBlisten\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-workers " " \fIn\fR
Override \fBworkers\fR: the number of SO_REUSEPORT listeners, or 0 for one per
CPU core.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print the usage summary and exit.
.PP
The three overrides are applied on top of the configuration file, so they win.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Configuration is TOML, by convention \fI/etc/samba/samba.toml\fR. Unknown keys
are rejected. See
.I samba.toml.example
for a fully commented file with every key.
.TP
.B listen
Bind address, default \fB"0.0.0.0:445"\fR. Binding 445 needs
CAP_NET_BIND_SERVICE or root.
.TP
.B workers
Listener goroutines, each with its own SO_REUSEPORT listener. 0 = one per CPU
core.
.TP
.B server_name
Advertised server name (\fB"SAMBA"\fR by default).
.TP
.B log_level
0 = warn, 1 = info, 2 = debug.
.TP
.B allow_guest
Allow unauthenticated guest sessions. Defaults to true only when no
\fB[[user]]\fR entries exist.
.TP
.B require_signing
Reject unsigned requests on authenticated sessions.
.TP
.B encrypt
Require SMB3 encryption on all post-authentication traffic. Default false;
encryption a client asks for (e.g. cifs \fBseal\fR) is honored either way.
.TP
.B prefer_aes256
Prefer AES-256 (GCM, then CCM) when the client offers it, instead of honoring
the client's preference order.
.TP
.B multichannel
Advertise SMB3 multichannel and accept session binding.
.TP
.B advertise_only
Addresses to advertise for multichannel (e.g. a dedicated storage NIC). Empty =
every non-loopback interface.
.TP
.B oplocks
Grant leases: read-caching, plus handle-caching when the client asks. Default
true. Write-caching is never granted.
.TP
.B max_connections
Concurrent connections the server will serve, default 512. Each connection
costs goroutines plus up to one maximum-size request frame of buffered data, so
this is the main lever on worst-case memory use. \fB\-1\fR removes the limit.
.TP
.B [[share]]
At least one is required: \fBname\fR, \fBpath\fR (an existing directory) and
\fBread_only\fR (default false). Authorization is share-level only, so
\fBread_only\fR applies to every user. The name \fIIPC$\fR is reserved.
.TP
.B [[user]]
\fBname\fR plus exactly one of \fBpassword\fR or \fBnt_hash\fR (32 hex
characters), checked by NTLMv2. There is no directory service behind it.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /etc/samba/samba.toml
Conventional configuration path.
.TP
.I samba.toml
Default when \fB\-\-config\fR is not given.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
.nf
# /etc/samba/samba.toml
listen = "0.0.0.0:445"
require_signing = true
multichannel = true

[[share]]
name = "data"
path = "/srv/data"

[[user]]
name = "alice"
password = "secret"
.fi
.PP
Check it before starting, and see what it will advertise:
.PP
.nf
samba --check --config /etc/samba/samba.toml
samba --list-interfaces --config /etc/samba/samba.toml
.fi
.PP
Run it:
.PP
.nf
samba --config /etc/samba/samba.toml
.fi
.PP
Mount it from a client:
.PP
.nf
mount -t cifs //server/data /mnt -o username=alice,password=secret,vers=3.1.1,sec=ntlmsspi
mount -t cifs //server/data /mnt -o username=alice,password=secret,vers=3.1.1,seal
mount -t cifs //server/data /mnt -o username=alice,password=secret,vers=3.1.1,multichannel,max_channels=4
.fi
.SH SIGNALS
.TP
.B SIGINT, SIGTERM
Stop the server. Listeners close, connections drain, and the workers are waited
for before the process exits with status 0.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Clean shutdown, or a successful reporting command.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error, a configuration error, or a startup failure.
.SH SECURITY
Authorization is \fBshare-level only\fR: \fBread_only\fR applies to every user,
and all file I/O runs as the server's Unix user. Role-based access, per-share
user lists and SID-to-uid mapping are not implemented. Run the server as a
dedicated unprivileged user that owns only the share trees. Symlinks inside a
share are followed even when they point outside it, as in Samba's \fBwide
links\fR. No external security review has been done; do not expose port 445 to
the public internet. See
.I SECURITY.md.
